/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex:30:Note that Feige's \RXOR hypothesis can be recovered by setting $\pred = \mathrm{\threexor}$.  Using this, we can weaken the assumptions for \pref{thm:r3xor-hardness} so that it only needs the Random $\ABEL$ Hypothesis to be true for some balanced pairwise independent subgroup $\pred$.
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex-31-
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex-32-\begin{theorem}\label{thm:abel}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex:33:Let $\pred$ be a predicate which is a balanced pairwise independent subgroup.  Assume the Random $\ABEL$ Hypothesis.  Then there is no polynomial-time algorithm for \robustgiso.  More precisely, there is an absolute constant $\delta >0$ such that the following holds: suppose there is a $t(n)$-time algorithm which can distinguish $(1-\eps)$-isomorphic $n$-vertex, $m$-edge graph pairs from pairs which are not even $(1-\delta)$-isomorphic (where $m = O(n)$).  Then there is a  universal constant $\Delta \in \Z^+$ and a $t(O(n))$-time algorithm which outputs ``typical'' for almost all $n$-variable, $\Delta n$-constraint instances of the $\ABEL$ problem, yet which never outputs ``typical'' on instances which are $(1-\eps)$-satisfiable.
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex-34-\end{theorem}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex-35-
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex-36-\paragraph{Outline of proof.}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/abelian.tex-37-The proof of Theorem~\pref{thm:abel} largely follows the same outline as the proof of \pref{thm:r3xor-hardness}.  As a result, our proof of \pref{thm:abel} will be carried out at a somewhat high level, except in those areas where we need to highlight changes.  The most significant change is that the general abelian group setting requires us to develop more complicated gadgets, and we will need to describe these gadgets before stating the reduction.
