/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-39-\begin{lemma}[SOS completeness]\label{lem:sos-completeness}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex:40:    Let $\calC$ be a \threexor instance on variable set $\calX$ and suppose that every SOS refutation of~$\calC$ requires  degree exceeding~$r$.  Then every SOS refutation of the statement ``$G_{\calC}$ and $G_{\homog{\calC}}$ are isomorphic'' requires degree exceeding~$r/3$.
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-41-\end{lemma}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-42-
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-43-\ifnum\full=1
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-44-\begin{proof}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex:45:    Since $\calC$ cannot be refuted in degree~$r$, there is a pseudo-expectation operator $\pE_{\calC}[\cdot]$ defined on the space of real polynomials of degree at most~$r$ over the indeterminates $(A[x \mapsto a])_{x \in \calX, a \in \Z_2}$ associated to~$\calC$.  This $\pE_{\calC}[\cdot]$ is normalized, linear, and satisfies:
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-46-    \begin{enumerate}[i.]
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex:47:        \item \label{pEC:zero-one} $\pE_{\calC}[(A[x \mapsto a]^2 - A[x \mapsto a]) \cdot q] = 0$ for all $x \in \calX$, $a \in \Z_2$, and polynomials~$q$;
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex:48:        \item \label{pEC:assignment} $\pE_{\calC}[(A[x \mapsto 0] + A[x \mapsto 1] - 1) \cdot q] = 0$ for all $x \in \calX$ and polynomials~$q$;
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex:49:        \item \label{pEC:constraints} $\pE_{\calC}[(\littlesum_{\alpha_C \text{\ satisfying $C$}} A[x_1 \mapsto \alpha_C(x_1)]A[x_2 \mapsto \alpha_C(x_2)]A[x_3 \mapsto \alpha_C(x_3)] - 1) \cdot q] = 0$ for each $C \in \calC$ involving variables $x_1, x_2, x_3$ and all polynomials~$q$;
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex:50:        \item \label{pEC:SOS} $\pE_{\calC}[p^2] \geq 0$ for all polynomials~$p$.
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-51-    \end{enumerate}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/completeness-alt.tex-52-
