/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex:41:    In this case we say that $G$ and $H$ are \emph{$\alpha$-isomorphic}.
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex-42-\end{definition}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex:43:Observe that this definition is symmetric in $G$ and $H$.  The two graphs are isomorphic if and only if they are $1$-isomorphic.  We will almost always\rnote{always?} consider the case that $G$ and $H$ have the same number of edges. We prove:
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex-44-\begin{theorem}                                     \label{thm:lasserre-gap}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex-45-    For infinitely many $n$, there exist pairs of $n$-vertex, $O(n)$-edge graphs~$G$ and~$H$ such that:
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex-46-    \begin{itemize}
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex:47:        \item $G$ and $H$ are not $(1-10^{-14})$-isomorphic;
/tmp/paper_1401_src/intro.tex:48:        \item any SOS refutation of the statement ``$G$ and $H$ are isomorphic''\footnote{When naturally encoded.} requires degree~$\Omega(n)$.
