The first triage point is decisive: the paper does not claim ordinary “permute a random graph and flip a few edges” instances are hard. Its hardness is conditional and distribution-specific, coming from a linear reduction of noisy random 3XOR into sparse graph gadgets; it also explicitly notes easy regimes (trees, dense additive-PTAS cases, and exact satisfiable XOR via Gaussian elimination). I’m now checking whether the reduction’s planted side can be generated without solving and whether its gadget permutation remains short enough for the answer cap.
